`@SpringBootTest` is appropriate for tests that verify **cross-cutting behavior** — how the full application hangs together.

```java
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)
class ApplicationSmokeTest {
    @Autowired TestRestTemplate restTemplate;

    @Test
    void healthEndpointReturnsUp() {
        ResponseEntity<String> response = restTemplate.getForEntity("/actuator/health", String.class);
        assertThat(response.getStatusCode()).isEqualTo(HttpStatus.OK);
    }
}
```

`RANDOM_PORT` starts the actual HTTP server — not MockMvc — on a random port. This tests the full HTTP stack including filters, exception handlers, and content negotiation. Use this for smoke tests, security integration tests, and scenarios where MockMvc's simulated request pipeline doesn't catch the issue.

`MOCK` (default) doesn't start a real server but provides a mock servlet environment. Combined with `@AutoConfigureMockMvc`, this gives you `MockMvc` in a full context — useful when you need all beans loaded but don't need a real HTTP server.

Reserve `@SpringBootTest` for the 10-20% of tests that genuinely need the full context. The other 80% should use slices.

A good MockMvc test verifies the full request-response contract: HTTP method, URL, request body, status code, response body, and headers.

```java
@WebMvcTest(UserController.class)
class UserControllerTest {
    @Autowired MockMvc mockMvc;
    @MockBean UserService userService;

    @Test
    void createUser_returnsCreatedWithLocation() throws Exception {
        when(userService.create(any())).thenReturn(new User(1L, "John", "john@test.com"));

        mockMvc.perform(post("/api/users")
                .contentType(APPLICATION_JSON)
                .content("{\\"name\\":\\"John\\",\\"email\\":\\"john@test.com\\"}"))
            .andExpect(status().isCreated())
            .andExpect(header().exists("Location"))
            .andExpect(jsonPath("$.id").value(1))
            .andExpect(jsonPath("$.name").value("John"));
    }

    @Test
    void createUser_withInvalidEmail_returns400() throws Exception {
        mockMvc.perform(post("/api/users")
                .contentType(APPLICATION_JSON)
                .content("{\\"name\\":\\"John\\",\\"email\\":\\"not-an-email\\"}"))
            .andExpect(status().isBadRequest())
            .andExpect(jsonPath("$.errors[0].field").value("email"));
    }
}
```

The validation test doesn't need the service mock configured — Spring's `@Valid` rejects the request before the controller method executes. This is important: **validation tests verify Spring's behavior, not your service logic.**

`jsonPath()` uses JSONPath expressions to assert on specific fields in the response body. For complex responses, `@JsonTest` with `JacksonTester` is more readable than deeply nested jsonPath assertions.

MockMvc's value extends beyond happy-path testing. Error handling, security, and custom Spring MVC components are where integration tests catch bugs that unit tests miss.

```java
@Test
void shouldReturn404ForUnknownUser() throws Exception {
    when(userService.findById(999L)).thenThrow(new UserNotFoundException(999L));

    mockMvc.perform(get("/api/users/999"))
        .andExpect(status().isNotFound())
        .andExpect(jsonPath("$.message").value("User 999 not found"));
}

@Test
@WithMockUser(roles = "ADMIN")
void adminCanDeleteUser() throws Exception {
    mockMvc.perform(delete("/api/users/1"))
        .andExpect(status().isNoContent());
}

@Test
@WithAnonymousUser
void anonymousCannotDeleteUser() throws Exception {
    mockMvc.perform(delete("/api/users/1"))
        .andExpect(status().isUnauthorized());
}
```

The `@WithMockUser` annotation creates a mock authentication — no real login required. It works with `@WebMvcTest` because Spring Security's auto-configuration is included in the web slice. Test both the allowed and denied paths — a security test that only verifies "admin can access" without verifying "non-admin cannot access" proves nothing.

`@ControllerAdvice` exception handling is tested implicitly: throw the exception from the mocked service, and verify that MockMvc returns the correct status code and error body. This catches `@ExceptionHandler` mapping issues that only surface with a running Spring context.

For file upload testing, MockMvc supports `MockMultipartFile`. For custom `HandlerMethodArgumentResolver` implementations (like resolving the current user from a JWT token), MockMvc tests verify that the resolver integrates correctly with the controller — something a plain unit test can't validate because argument resolution requires the Spring MVC pipeline.

```java
@WebMvcTest(AdminController.class)
class AdminControllerSecurityTest {
    @Autowired MockMvc mockMvc;
    @MockBean AdminService adminService;

    @Test
    @WithMockUser(roles = "ADMIN")
    void adminCanAccessDashboard() throws Exception {
        mockMvc.perform(get("/admin/dashboard"))
            .andExpect(status().isOk());
    }

    @Test
    @WithMockUser(roles = "USER")
    void regularUserCannotAccessAdmin() throws Exception {
        mockMvc.perform(get("/admin/dashboard"))
            .andExpect(status().isForbidden());
    }

    @Test
    @WithAnonymousUser
    void anonymousIsRedirectedToLogin() throws Exception {
        mockMvc.perform(get("/admin/dashboard"))
            .andExpect(status().isUnauthorized());
    }
}
```

Three tests, three roles, three expected outcomes. The test class verifies the complete security rule. `@WithMockUser` defaults to username "user" and role "USER" if no parameters are specified — useful for endpoints that just require authentication without specific roles.

For JWT-based security or custom `UserDetails` implementations, create a custom annotation that builds the appropriate authentication object. `@WithSecurityContext` lets you define a factory that populates the `SecurityContext` with your custom principal, authorities, and claims.

For testing CSRF protection, MockMvc provides `csrf()` post-processor: `mockMvc.perform(post("/api/orders").with(csrf()))`. Without it, POST/PUT/DELETE requests fail with 403 when CSRF is enabled. Testing CSRF explicitly ensures your security configuration handles both stateless API endpoints (CSRF disabled) and stateful web endpoints (CSRF enabled) correctly.

Controller-level security (`@RequestMapping` + security config) is tested via MockMvc. Method-level security (`@PreAuthorize`, `@Secured` on service methods) requires a Spring context because the security proxy wraps the bean.

```java
@SpringBootTest
class OrderServiceSecurityTest {
    @Autowired OrderService orderService;

    @Test
    @WithMockUser(roles = "MANAGER")
    void managerCanApproveOrder() {
        assertDoesNotThrow(() -> orderService.approve(1L));
    }

    @Test
    @WithMockUser(roles = "USER")
    void regularUserCannotApprove() {
        assertThrows(AccessDeniedException.class, () -> orderService.approve(1L));
    }
}
```

The `@PreAuthorize("hasRole('MANAGER')")` annotation on the service method is enforced by a Spring AOP proxy. Without a Spring context, the proxy doesn't exist and the method executes without authorization checks. This is why method-level security tests require `@SpringBootTest` — the proxy must be active for the security rule to apply.

Always test the **denied** path. An `@PreAuthorize` that's misconfigured (wrong SpEL expression, missing `@EnableMethodSecurity`) silently allows everything. The denied-path test catches this: if `assertThrows(AccessDeniedException.class)` doesn't throw, the security rule isn't active.

For OAuth2 resource servers, use `SecurityMockMvcRequestPostProcessors.jwt()` to create mock JWT tokens: `mockMvc.perform(get("/api/data").with(jwt().authorities(new SimpleGrantedAuthority("SCOPE_read"))))`. This tests that your security rules correctly inspect JWT claims and scopes without needing a running authorization server.

